Minimal repro at /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-tcl-lsp/b4c77bf3-47fd-5ba4-822a-000fa233bcc1/scratchpad/audit/14/minimal/t.tcl:
```
oo::class create A {
    method make {} { return [B new] }
}
oo::class create B {
    method greet {} { return "hi" }
}
set a [A new]
set b [$a make]
$b greet
```
Oracle: `tclsh9.0 t.tcl` and `tclsh8.6 t.tcl` both exit 0 with no error (B's `greet` runs fine — this is fully valid, deterministic, non-ambiguous Tcl).
LSP: `cd .../audit/14/minimal && python3 lsp_client.py diagnostics t.tcl` ->
`WARNING W307 8:0-8:2 Non-literal command name — cannot statically analyze` (flags `$b greet` on line 8, 0-based).
Cross-check that the LSP *could* have resolved it: `python3 lsp_client.py definition t.tcl 7 10` (the `make` call on line 7) -> `Definition (1 locations): t.tcl 1:11-1:15` (correctly finds `::A::make`), and `hover t.tcl 7 10` -> `**method** \`::A::make\` (0 param(s))`. But `python3 lsp_client.py definition t.tcl 8 3` (the `greet` call) -> `Definition (0 locations)` — the type of `$b` was never propagated from `make`'s return, so the resolvable inherited/returned-object case silently degrades into a false "cannot analyze" diagnostic plus a missed go-to-definition, rather than either (a) correctly resolving it, which the underlying literal-`[B new]`-return makes trivially possible, or (b) abstaining without noise as it does for the genuinely-undecidable `{*}$element` case. Same bug reproduces in the fuller, corpus-faithful repro at .../audit/14/repro.tcl and .../audit/14/mini7/t.tcl (structurally mirroring generalClasses.tcl's `NgspiceParser`/`Parser`/`Netlist` class shape, `buildTopNetlist`'s `{*}$element` dispatch, and a caller chaining `set result [$parser buildTopNetlist $netlistLines]; ... [$result report] ...`), where it instead flags the *first* call (`$parser buildTopNetlist ...`, also proven independently resolvable via `definition` -> Parser::buildTopNetlist) rather than the second — confirming the false flag is a generic collateral effect of a later chained `$var method` dispatch on a return-typed variable existing anywhere later in the file, not tied to which of the two calls is "first."

