    source [file join $dir Vector3d.tcl]

}} $dir]`; testpkg/Vector3d.tcl defines `oo::class create testpkg::mathvec3d::Vector3d` with a `getX` method + `package provide testpkg 1.0`; main.tcl does `lappend auto_path .../testpkg; package require testpkg; set v [testpkg::mathvec3d::Vector3d new 1 2 3]; puts [$v getX]`. tclsh9.0/tclsh8.6 both print `1`. LSP: `diagnostics main.tcl` and `diagnostics testpkg/pkgIndex.tcl` both 0 items; `definition main.tcl 3 27` (0-based, on `Vector3d`) -> `Vector3d.tcl 6:17-6:45` (exact `oo::class create testpkg::mathvec3d::Vector3d` span); `references testpkg/Vector3d.tcl 6 30` -> finds both the definition and the main.tcl call site. To isolate PackageResolver's own contribution (vs. blanket workspace-file scanning, which would find same-workspace files regardless of package correctness), I re-ran with the package reachable ONLY via `.tcl-lsp.ini`'s `[project] libraryPaths` (outside the workspace root) and a bare unqualified proc `greetPkg` defined in the apply-sourced file: with `package require baretest` present, LSP diagnostics = 0 items (W123 correctly suppressed, matches tclsh printing "hello"); with the `package require` line removed, LSP fires `HINT W123 0:6-0:14 Unknown command 'greetPkg'` (matches tclsh's real `invalid command name "greetPkg"` error). Both deterministic across repeated runs and both tclsh8.6/9.0. Two unrelated, pre-existing gaps noted but explicitly NOT attributed to this finding since they reproduce identically with a trivial non-apply, non-package `source`: (1) hover returns nothing at a cross-file call site generally (works at same-file call sites and at the definition site itself); (2) `definition`/`references` never reach into externally-configured library paths at all (only diagnostics consult PackageResolver for that) — confirmed identical with the simplest `package ifneeded testpkg 1.0 [list source [file join $dir Vector3d.tcl]]` form, so it is not specific to the apply/list/$dir mechanic under test.

root_cause_hint:
rust/tcl-lexer/src/lexer.rs ~lines 636-651, the `${name}` branch of the Var-token lexer:
  let content_empty = self.pos == content_start;
  let has_close_brace = self.current_byte() == Some(b'}');
  let span_end = if content_empty && has_close_brace { self.pos + 1 } else { self.pos };
`span_end` only advances past the closing `}` for the degenerate empty-name case (`${}`); the ordinary non-empty case leaves `span_end = self.pos`, i.e. one byte short of the `}` that `self.pos += 1` (a few lines later) separately consumes for continued lexing — so the emitted `Token`'s own span never covers the closing brace. That short span is taken verbatim as the read span in tcl-compiler/src/analyser/scope.rs::record_arg_var_reads (`reads.push((name, tok.span))`), lands in `VarDef.references`, flows through tcl-lsp-core/src/definition.rs::linked_var_reference_spans into tcl-lsp-core/src/rename.rs::rename_var, which uses it directly as `TextEdit.range` while `build_var_ref_replacement` (rename.rs:1254, `text.strip_prefix("${")` / `rest.strip_suffix('}').unwrap_or(rest)`) assumes the span was the *whole* token and unconditionally formats `"${{{new_tail}}}"` — appending its own closing brace on top of the untouched original one. The existing tests (rename_var_preserves_braced_reference_form and siblings, rename.rs:1406-1462) only assert the edit list's `new_text` values contain the expected string and never apply (range, new_text) back onto the source to check the result parses/runs, which is why this shipped uncaught.
